---

A **region** is a part of the page with its own rules for rendering and for waking. It is the only primitive in ogygia. Each marked import compiles to the same `<Region>` envelope: an island, a server island, a lake, a held region. They are not four features. They are four settings of one thing.

::: tip Why one primitive
One envelope means one way to hydrate, one way to sign, one way to pass props, and one mental model. When you understand a region, you understand all of them. The remainder of this section is only the settings.
:::

## The two dials

Two independent dials configure a region. You set them as import attributes:

| Dial | Question it answers | Values |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `render` | Where does the HTML come from, and when? | inline in the SSR pass of the page (default) · `'deferred'`: fetched from a signed endpoint on each request |
| `wake` | Does this ship client JS, and when does it run? | none (default, HTML only) · `'load'` · `'idle'` · `'visible'` · `'interaction'` · a media query · `'none'` (frozen by intent) |

Each combination is legal. Each named kind is one point in that grid:

## The kinds

| Kind | Mark | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Client island](/docs/regions/client-islands) | `with { wake: 'visible' }` | SSRs inline. Hydrates on a schedule. The interactive one. |
| [Server island](/docs/regions/server-islands) | `with { render: 'deferred' }` | HTML rendered on each fetch from a signed endpoint. Fresh on each request. Streams into a static shell. Can wake after arrival. |
| [Lake](/docs/regions/lakes) | `with { wake: 'none' }` | Frozen server HTML. Ships zero JS. Continues unchanged through SPA swaps. |
| [Held region](/docs/regions/held-regions) | `region(Comp, props)` | A region as a **value**: made in a load or a remote function, sent over the wire, placed with `<Region of={value} />`. |

Unmarked markup is not a region. It is plain server HTML compiled into the page. That is the default, and the least costly thing on the site.

## What each region guarantees

The settings can differ. The contract of the envelope does not:

- **SSR first.** A region renders real HTML on the server. JS, if there is JS, attaches to that HTML. It never replaces it. Set JS off, and the markup remains.
- **One hydration.** A region hydrates one time. It adopts its server DOM in place. There is no discard-and-recreate, and no flash. Nested regions become part of the single hydration of their parent.
- **Signed capabilities.** All later fetches of a region (a deferred hole, an SWR remount) go through an endpoint with an HMAC capability. The capability is made at SSR time. The client holds a ticket, not an open door.
- **Props cross by value.** Captured host values cross the boundary through devalue: plain data, `Map`/`Set`/`Date`, and your registered [transportables](/docs/data-state/state-context). Functions and promises do not cross. State that must stay live, lives inside the region.

## Shared mechanics

Two pages in this section apply to each kind:

- [Timing & nesting](/docs/regions/timing-nesting): the full `wake` schedule reference, `visible` margins, and regions that contain regions.
- [Client islands](/docs/regions/client-islands): mark an import with `wake`, and it ships JS on a schedule. The marked import is a real component binding. You can pass it, render it dynamically, or put it in an `{#each}`.

From here, read the kind you need. Or start with [client islands](/docs/regions/client-islands). Most pages use that kind first.

